Answer: ```java
return sends the control back to the place from where it came. In the main method, the control came from the JVM, so as soon as return executes, the control goes back to the JVM and the program ends. The loop below could run more than once, but the statement after the print is return, so the message is printed only once.
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
for (int i = 1; i < 3; i++) {
System.out.println("StringStack Technologies");
return;
}
}
}
```

Output: StringStack Technologies

Answer: The control enters the loop, prints Hello, and break immediately takes the control out of the loop. The statement after the loop then prints World. The loop does not run again.

```java
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("Hello");
break;
}
System.out.println("World");
}
}
```

Output: Hello World

Answer: ```java
continue takes the control back to the loop, skipping the remaining statements of the current iteration. The loop then moves on with the next iteration (increment and condition check). The difference from break is that break ends the loop completely, while continue only skips the rest of the current iteration. In the program below, "How are you" is skipped when i is 2.
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
System.out.println("Hello");
if (i == 2) {
continue;
}
System.out.println("How are you");
}
}
}
```

Output: Hello How are you Hello Hello How are you

Answer: The outer loop repeats 3 times. In every iteration it prints Hello, then the inner loop prints World twice, and then How are you is printed. So the group Hello, World, World, How are you is repeated three times.

```java
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
System.out.println("Hello");
for (int j = 1; j <= 2; j++) {
System.out.println("World");
}
System.out.println("How are you");
}
}
}
```

Output: Hello World World How are you Hello World World How are you Hello World World How are you

Answer: ```java
break brings the control out of the loop in which it is present, so it comes out of the inner loop only, and the outer loop continues. In the program below the inner loop is written for 3 repetitions, but when j becomes 2 the break executes, so the inner loop runs only 2 times (World is printed twice). The output is exactly the same as in the previous program, although the loop was written differently.
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
System.out.println("Hello");
for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
System.out.println("World");
if (j == 2) {
break;
}
}
System.out.println("How are you");
}
}
}
```

Output: Hello World World How are you Hello World World How are you Hello World World How are you

